## §C — Professional code + GitHub/web + META + packages (your paragraph C, enhanced)

> *You asked:* always produce professional quality code as if for a big company app/project; use GitHub and the web for references, assets, resources and code; always use the best latest META methods for implementations and planning; component libraries, npm / Dotnet packages and more.

This was one paragraph with **four** requirements. Each is a full sub-law.

### C.1 Professional quality code — big-company bar

**MUST**

- Write as if senior engineers at a serious company will review and ship it: clear names, types where the stack expects them, bounded errors, no silent `catch`, no stubs on user paths.
- **Root-cause fixes**, not bandaids (hide races with timers, “refresh all” to mask cache bugs, delete libraries to avoid fixing them).
- Scoped, coherent diffs; match repo conventions; no parallel architecture for one feature.
- Preserve proven interaction systems (drag/drop, selection, virtualization, IPC). Extend; do not silently rewrite.
- Maintainable structure: focused modules, obvious data flow, testable boundaries where the repo already tests.
- Basic a11y/keyboard on real interactive surfaces (focus, labels, Esc/Enter where expected).
- Comments only for invariants / non-obvious intent / protect-notes.

**MUST NOT**

- Leave `TODO`, “Coming soon,” placeholders, or fake data on in-scope user paths.
- Duplicate near-identical components/services instead of extending the real one.
- Claim production-ready while build/type/runtime failures remain.
- Commit secrets, machine-specific junk, or debug dumps into product code.

### C.2 GitHub + the web — references, assets, resources, and code

**MUST look outward** before/during non-trivial work. Memory is not ground truth.

| Need | Agent action |
|------|----------------|
| **References** (UX/architecture) | Study competing products, exemplar apps, design galleries, official architecture docs |
| **Assets** | §B.3 generate or fetch PNG/SVG packs; GitHub `raw` when license-clear |
| **Resources** | RFCs, API references, package docs, changelogs, migration guides, design tokens, engineering notes |
| **Code** | Find maintained, permissively licensed GitHub examples/modules; **adapt** into repo conventions — do not vendor-dump trees |

**Also MUST when**

- Planning or redesigning → how strong products/repos solve it now  
- Implementing subsystems → libs + exemplar repos + official docs  
- Debugging → exact errors, Issues/PRs, release notes  
- Choosing packages → registry truth for **current** stable versions  

**MUST NOT**

- Copy large copyrighted/proprietary material verbatim  
- Dump vendor trees and call it integration  
- Cite imaginary APIs/versions — verify on docs/registry  
- Ignore licenses when adapting code or assets  

### C.3 Best latest META methods — implementations **and** planning

**META** = the best **current, proven** approach — not the first hack that compiles, and not a plan that ignores how serious products build the thing.

**META for implementation — MUST**

- Prefer battle-tested libraries/APIs over thin hand-rolls for non-trivial domains (FS/shell, metadata, archives, imaging, search, IPC, UI, parsers, caches, graphics).
- Prefer **modern stable** APIs and maintained majors; read breaking-change notes; do not copy stale blog patterns when the lib has a newer recommended API.
- Deepen real product surfaces (list, preview, search, settings, plugins) rather than forbidden iframe/sidecar/HTTP theater when project rules ban that.
- Choose scalable approaches when user-visible/perf-sensitive: pooling, cancellation, virtualization, incremental work, GPU/compositor-friendly paths.
- If several paths exist: pick correct + maintainable + architecture-aligned — then implement **fully**.

**META for planning — MUST**

- Research beat first (docs + GitHub + package ecosystems) before proposing/executing non-trivial plans.
- Every serious plan names: **(1) META stack** (libs/packages/APIs), **(2) user-visible integration surface**, **(3) proof** (builds/checks).
- Reject plans that are teardown-only, cosmetic-only, or “skin a stub and call it META.”

**MUST NOT** call cosmetic skins or stubs “META.”

### C.4 Component libraries, npm, Dotnet packages, and more

Package/component ecosystems are **first-class tools**, not an afterthought.

| Layer | Agent expectation |
|-------|-------------------|
| **Component libraries / UI primitives** | Use mature headless or styled libraries aligned with the repo (or newly justified): accessible primitives, virtualizers, canvases/flow, menus, charts, etc. Restyle to product tokens — do not drop a second conflicting design system without an explicit decision. |
| **npm (JS/TS)** | Well-maintained packages for real problems; confirm current stable on the registry before wiring. |
| **NuGet / .NET (Dotnet)** | Proven packages in the project ecosystem (shell, metadata, imaging, archives, IPC helpers, etc.); bump carefully with release notes. |
| **And more** | Same bar for any stack the repo uses (crates, pip, platform SDKs, WinRT/Win32, WebView2, etc.): maintained, license-clear, justified, current. |

**MUST NOT**

- Add vanity dependencies  
- Drive-by major upgrades across the tree unless the task scopes that  
- Fight the repo with a conflicting component library skin  

```text
❌ BAD: hand-roll fragile core; skip GitHub; plan with adjectives only; ignore npm/NuGet
✅ GOOD: research exemplar + registry → pick component lib / npm / NuGet META path → adapt code → deepen real surface → prove with builds
```

